# Full-Stack Developer Roadmap (MERN)

> Your original note was a great **first-project checklist**. This expands it into a complete **developer roadmap**, kept in three layers: build the foundations, build your project (your original plan), then become job-ready.
>
> Your original 6 points are marked with ⭐ so you can see they're all still here — just placed in the right context.

---

## Layer 1 — Foundations (before you build anything)

Learn these first. Everything else stands on top of them.

- [ ] **HTML** — structure, semantic tags, forms
- [ ] **CSS** — flexbox, grid, responsive design, basic animations
- [ ] **JavaScript (deeply)** — variables, functions, arrays/objects, ES6+ (arrow functions, destructuring, spread), `map`/`filter`/`reduce`
- [ ] **Async JavaScript** — promises, `async/await`, `fetch()` — *critical for talking to APIs*
- [ ] **How the web works** — HTTP methods (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE), status codes, request/response cycle, JSON
- [ ] **Git & GitHub** — commit, push, pull, branches, merge — *non-negotiable, every job expects it*
- [ ] **Terminal basics** — navigating folders, running commands
- [ ] **React fundamentals** — components, props, state, `useState`, `useEffect`, handling events

*Rough time: 6–10 weeks depending on your starting point.*

---

## Layer 2 — Build Your Project (your original plan)

This is your handwritten note, in order. It's a solid sequence — frontend first with fake data, then wire up the real backend.

### Frontend
- [ ] ⭐ **Make a React application**
  - [ ] ⭐ Work on dummy data
  - [ ] ⭐ Hard-code the data first (get the UI working before the backend exists)
- [ ] **State management** — Context API (or Redux once the app grows) *(added)*

### Backend
- [ ] ⭐ **Create a backend** (Node.js + Express)
  - [ ] ⭐ Implement APIs
  - [ ] ⭐ Make those APIs RESTful
- [ ] **Middleware** — understand how Express middleware works *(added)*
- [ ] **Environment variables** — use `.env` files, keep secrets out of code *(added)*
- [ ] **Input validation & error handling** — validate requests, fail gracefully *(added)*

### Database
- [ ] ⭐ **Create MongoDB**
  - [ ] ⭐ How to set up MongoDB
  - [ ] ⭐ How to do CRUD operations
  - [ ] ⭐ Integrate with backend
  - [ ] ⭐ Check API response
- [ ] **Schema / data modeling** — use Mongoose, design your schemas *(added)*
- [ ] **Relationships between collections** *(added)*

### Wire it all together
- [ ] ⭐ **Integrate the DB with the backend**
- [ ] ⭐ **Integrate the backend with the frontend**
- [ ] ⭐ **Handle the API on the client side**
- [ ] ⭐ **Show the real data in the frontend** (replace the hard-coded data)
- [ ] ⭐ **Make the design good**

### The one big thing your note skipped
- [ ] **Authentication & authorization** *(added — this is the most important addition)*
  - [ ] Signup / login
  - [ ] Password hashing (bcrypt)
  - [ ] JWT or sessions
  - [ ] Protected routes
  - > Almost every real app and every job interview involves this.

- [ ] **Security basics** — CORS, sanitizing input, not exposing secrets *(added)*

*Rough time: 6–10 weeks to build a full project end-to-end.*

---

## Layer 3 — Job-Ready (after the project works)

Building one project isn't enough to get hired. This layer makes you employable.

- [ ] **Deployment** *(added — as important as authentication)*
  - [ ] Frontend → Vercel or Netlify
  - [ ] Backend → Render or Railway
  - [ ] Database → MongoDB Atlas
  - > "It works on my machine" doesn't count. Employers want to see a live link.

- [ ] **Testing** — at least the basics (Jest, or React Testing Library) *(added)*
- [ ] **Build 2–3 more projects** — each one harder than the last; variety matters more than one huge app
- [ ] **Polish your GitHub** — clean READMEs, live demo links, consistent commits
- [ ] **Optional but valuable:** TypeScript, Docker basics, CI/CD concepts, an intro to system design

*Rough time: ongoing — this is where you keep growing.*

